Definition:

Optional is a container object that may or may not contain a non-null value.
It is used to avoid NullPointerException and represent absence of value explicitly.

Why Optional?
Before Java 8:
if (user != null && user.getAddress() != null) ...

After Java 8:
Optional.ofNullable(user)
        .map(User::getAddress)
        .ifPresent(System.out::println);


✔ Cleaner
✔ Null-safe
✔ Functional style

Creating Optional
1️⃣ of()
Definition:

Creates Optional with non-null value.

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Sai");


⚠ Throws NPE if null.

2️⃣ ofNullable()
Definition:
Creates Optional that may hold null.
Optional<String> opt = Optional.ofNullable(null);

3️⃣ empty()
Definition:
Creates empty Optional.
Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();


Core Optional Methods (All Important)
1️⃣ isPresent()
Definition:
Checks if value exists.
Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Java");
System.out.println(opt.isPresent());

Output:
true

2️⃣ ifPresent()
Definition:
Executes action if value present.
opt.ifPresent(v -> System.out.println(v));

Output:
Java

3️⃣ get()
Definition:
Returns value if present.
opt.get();
⚠ Throws NoSuchElementException if empty.

👉 Interview: avoid get().

4️⃣ orElse()
Definition:
Returns value or default.
Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
System.out.println(opt.orElse("Default"));

Output:
Default

5️⃣ orElseGet()
Definition:
Returns value or supplier result.
opt.orElseGet(() -> "Generated");

6️⃣ orElseThrow()
Definition:
Throws exception if empty.
opt.orElseThrow(() -> new RuntimeException("No value"));

7️⃣ map()
Definition:
Transforms value if present.
Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("java");
System.out.println(opt.map(String::toUpperCase));

Output:
Optional[JAVA]

8️⃣ flatMap()
Definition:
Transforms Optional-returning function.
Optional<User> user = Optional.of(new User("Sai"));
Optional<String> name =
    user.flatMap(u -> Optional.of(u.getName()));

9️⃣ filter()
Definition:
Returns Optional if condition true.
Optional<Integer> opt = Optional.of(10);
System.out.println(opt.filter(n -> n > 5));

Output:
Optional[10]



Find Any
Optional<Integer> any =
    List.of(5,6,7).stream().findAny();

Max
Optional<Integer> max =
    List.of(3,9,2).stream().max(Integer::compare);

System.out.println(max);

Output:
Optional[9]

Using Optional in Stream Chain
String result =
    Optional.ofNullable("java")
            .map(String::toUpperCase)
            .orElse("NA");

System.out.println(result);

Output:
JAVA

Optional in Spring Boot (Real Usage)
Repository
Optional<User> findById(Long id);

Service
User user = repo.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() ->
                    new RuntimeException("Not found"));


✔ Standard practice

Optional Best Practices (Interview)

✔ Use as return type
✔ Avoid in fields
✔ Avoid in method parameters
✔ Avoid get()
✔ Prefer map / orElse

Interview Questions & Answers (Sequential)
Q1: What is Optional?
Answer:
Optional is a container object representing presence or absence of a value.

Q2: Why Optional introduced?
Answer:
To avoid NullPointerException and make null handling explicit.

Q3: Difference between of() and ofNullable()?
Answer:
of() rejects null; ofNullable() allows null.

Q4: Difference between orElse and orElseGet?
Answer:
orElse evaluates immediately; orElseGet lazily.

Q5: Why get() is discouraged?
Answer:
It throws exception if value absent.

Q6: Can Optional hold null?
Answer:
No. Optional either has value or empty.

Q7: Is Optional serializable?
Answer:
No.

Q8: Is Optional thread-safe?
Answer:
Yes, immutable.

Q9: Can Optional be field?
Answer:
Not recommended.

Q10: When should Optional be used?
Answer:
As return type when value may be absent.

Tricky Interview Questions (4+ Years)
🔥 Q11: Optional vs Null — performance?
Answer:
Optional adds small overhead; use for clarity not primitives-heavy loops.

🔥 Q12: Optional vs Exception?
Answer:
Optional → expected absence
Exception → exceptional condition

🔥 Q13: Why Optional not for parameters?
Answer:
Makes API confusing and unnecessary wrapping.

🔥 Q14: Can Optional contain Optional?
Answer:
Yes, but use flatMap() to avoid nesting.

🔥 Q15: Optional in collections?
Answer:
Avoid List<Optional<T>>.

🔥 Q16: Optional in entity?
Answer:
Not recommended (serialization/JPA issues).

🔥 Q17: Optional vs Stream?
Answer:
Optional → single value
Stream → multiple values

🔥 Q18: Best Optional interview one-liner?
Answer:

“Optional represents absence of value explicitly and avoids NullPointerException using functional style operations.”
